    "summary": "Review product (action=review) for direct check 3052d8d823d14854a617fe006f2a30a5, preflight-validated with valid=True and no diagnostics.\n\nWhat it addresses. The fixed check was accepted and executed fresh (experiment 41bb5656bcc7426dab29aae5e648536f, exit 0), and it reproduced the observed delivery gap: scenario declared fsm_implements_batching=true and fsm_implements_configuration_store=true; the AddNonvoter configuration entry committed at index 4 and reached the FSM through ApplyBatch (indices [1,4]); store_configuration_calls was 0 with entry_delivered_to_fsm true. Two open applicability issues recorded the same input conflict (one on the claim's grounding, one on the harness legality), so both are resolved here with attributed answers rather than being restated elsewhere.\n\nReview items. checker_correspondence (no_issue_found, empty counterevidence): the written ConfigurationStore contract (configuration.go:44-52) matches the encoded predicate store_configuration_called eq true; caller duties (leader elected, own-term entry committed, AddNonvoter commits under the existing quorum) are discharged; identity op_id cfg-1 is shared across prerequisite and result; the oracle is the emitted event, not tests_passed; the Barrier queued after the configuration entry guarantees the FSM drained it before sampling; exit 0. The competing ApplyBatch statement is recorded under limitations, not as contrary evidence. applicability (no_issue_found): the antecedent comes from runtime interface assertions and a committed-entry prerequisite, so applicability is fixed independently of the compared value.\n\nResolutions. Both issues are answered by attributing each statement to its owner and fixing which written responsibility the check encodes; each condition is disposed as independent_scope with sources, and the outer question (whether ConfigurationStore covers batching FSMs, or whether implementing both interfaces is even supported) is carried as an explicit scope limitation rather than renamed away or suppressed. The original conflict is preserved in the limitations and in the plan's harness alternatives.\n\nRemaining open: the contract question itself (which interface governs configuration persistence for batching FSMs) is retained as a limitation, not resolved by this review; no map change was made."
